.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-ENROLL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-enroll
.Nd put a virtual machine on the WireGuard mesh and in the estate inventory, whatever made it
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar vm
.Op Fl -ip Ar address
.Op Fl -role Ar role
.Op Fl -cluster Ar cluster
.Op Fl -id Ar n
.Op Fl -no-wg
.Nm
.Fl -all
.Op Fl -role Ar role
.Op Fl -cluster Ar cluster
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
For the libvirt domain
.Ar vm ,
.Nm :
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
finds its address, through
.Xr kldload-vm-ip 8 ,
unless
.Fl -ip
gives it;
.It
records it in the state database, with its role and cluster, which is
what the Ansible inventory is made from;
.It
over ssh as root, sets the guest's host name to
.Ar vm ,
gives it a mesh node id and runs
.Ql kube-network init
there \(em carrying the host's own
.Ql kube-network
across when the guest has WireGuard but not the script;
.It
peers the host and the guest with each other on both mesh planes,
.Ql 10.250.0. Ns Ar id
and
.Ql 10.251.0. Ns Ar id ,
checks that the host's mesh now carries the guest's address, and records
the enrolment so that
.Xr kvm-delete 8
can take it off the mesh again.
.El
.Pp
A guest that already has a node id keeps it, and only missing peers are
added: running it again is how an enrolment is repaired.
Ids are given from 150 up, clear of the bands
.Xr kube-cluster 8
uses; the host is 100.
.Pp
It refuses a golden \(em by name, or by an
.Ql @golden
snapshot of its volume \(em since a golden's key and id would be copied
into every clone.
It leaves a node of a Kubernetes cluster that
.Xr kube-cluster 8
has not yet put on the mesh to it, since that tool gives the control
plane id 1.
.Pp
It runs itself under
.Xr sudo 8 .
The names and values it is given are checked before that: the name
becomes the guest's host name and all of them reach commands run as root
on the guest.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -all
Enrol every running virtual machine, one at a time, and print one line
for each that did not enrol, with why, then the counts.
.Pa kldload-enroll-sweep.timer
runs this three minutes after boot and every ten minutes after, so a
machine made by any tool is enrolled once it has an address.
.It Fl -ip Ar address
The guest's IPv4 address, for a machine libvirt's leases do not know.
.It Fl -role Ar role , Fl -cluster Ar cluster
Its role and cluster in the inventory.
Without
.Fl -role
a machine keeps the role it has; a new one is
.Ql vm .
.It Fl -id Ar n
This mesh node id, instead of the next free one.
.It Fl -no-wg
The inventory only; no mesh.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the header comment and exit 0.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/lib/kldload/mesh/enrolled/ Ns Ar vm
The machine's node id and public key.
.It Pa /root/.ssh/id_ed25519
The key it reaches guests with.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Enrolled, or already enrolled.
.It 1
No address for the machine.
.It 2
A usage error; nothing was attempted.
.It 3
The machine is a golden; or the mesh step failed after the inventory row
was written.
.It 4
A cluster node left to
.Xr kube-cluster 8 .
.El
.Pp
With
.Fl -all :
0 when every machine it tried is on the mesh, 1 when any ended
incomplete; one with no address, a golden or a cluster node does not
count against it.
.Sh EXAMPLES
On a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30, with no machines running:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ journalctl -u kldload-enroll-sweep.service -n 4 -o cat
Starting kldload-enroll-sweep.service - Enrol every running VM into the kldload estate (name, mesh, inventory)...
[enroll] sweep: 0 on the mesh, 0 without an address, 0 incomplete, 0 golden(s) left alone, 0 cluster node(s) left to kube-cluster
kldload-enroll-sweep.service: Deactivated successfully.
Finished kldload-enroll-sweep.service - Enrol every running VM into the kldload estate (name, mesh, inventory).
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
What it refuses before doing anything, with this release's
.Nm
first on the path:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-enroll k8s-golden
[enroll] k8s-golden is a golden (by name or its @golden snapshot) -- not enrolling it: a golden's mesh identity would be copied into every clone
# (exited 3)
$ kldload-enroll 'bad;name'
not a usable VM name (it becomes the guest's hostname): bad;name
# (exited 2)
$ kldload-enroll web01 --id 12x
--id needs a number: 12x
# (exited 2)
$ kldload-enroll web01 --ip
--ip needs a value (try --help)
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Pp
Enrolling; not run for this page, since no machine was running:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-enroll web01 --role web --cluster shop
# kldload-enroll --all
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kfire 8 ,
.Xr kldload-vm-ip 8 ,
.Xr kspawn 8 ,
.Xr kube-cluster 8 ,
.Xr kvm-clone 8 ,
.Xr kvm-create 8 ,
.Xr kvm-delete 8
.Sh HISTORY
Until 2026-08-22 only
.Xr kube-cluster 8
put machines on the mesh; everything else made them and stopped.
That day the first sweep enrolled one machine of ten and reported
success: each enrolment read the rest of the sweep's list as its input.
On 2026-09-23 the sweep enrolled a golden while it was being built, and
six cluster nodes cloned from it shared one key and one id; later that
day it numbered three cluster nodes before
.Xr kube-cluster 8
could, the control plane among them, and the cluster lost its API
address.
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: an option given last with no value
failed as an unbound variable, exit 1 \(em the code for a machine with
no address.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Enrolling needs ssh to the guest as root with the host's key, which
kldload's goldens carry; a machine made some other way is recorded in the
inventory and left off the mesh, with exit 3.
